<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Native-AOT sibling of tests/TrimProbe, and it exists to answer a question this repository has
    so far refused to answer dishonestly.

    src/DocToolkit deliberately does NOT set IsAotCompatible. CLAUDE.md records why: the claim was
    never verified, because the machine it was attempted on had no platform linker, so ILC never
    ran. "An unverified compatibility claim is worse than an absent one." This project plus the
    aot-guard job are what would let the claim be made - or refused, with evidence.

    WHY IT SHARES TrimProbe's Program.cs RATHER THAN COPYING IT. The two probes must exercise the
    same capabilities and assert the same results, or the weaker guard silently covers less than the
    stronger one. A copy drifts the first time somebody adds a capability to one of them; the same
    reasoning that puts SetCellValue and SectionPropertiesFactory in one place each.

    DELIBERATELY NOT IN DocToolkit.sln - identical reasoning to TrimProbe, and stronger here. An AOT
    publish is SelfContained and emits native output, so ci.yml's "zero *.so under any bin/" premise
    guard would fail on the .NET runtime itself and read exactly like a real breach.

    ProjectReference, not PackageReference: the question is "is the code in THIS pull request
    AOT-safe", which only a project reference can answer.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!--
      AOT is strictly stronger than trimming: it implies PublishTrimmed and SelfContained, and it
      additionally forbids runtime code generation. ClosedXML, OpenXML and OfficeIMO are all
      reflection-heavy, so this failing is a genuinely possible - and useful - outcome. A failure
      here is evidence that IsAotCompatible must STAY unset, which is worth as much as a pass.
    -->
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>

    <!--
      Same reasoning as TrimProbe's: left at its default the toolchain collapses a whole assembly's
      warnings into one, which cannot be attributed to a call site - so a new unsafe pattern in
      DocToolkit would be indistinguishable from the ClosedXML warning already known about.
    -->
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>

    <!--
      Opts out of the root's TreatWarningsAsErrors for exactly TrimProbe's reason: AOT reports
      IL/AOT warnings from the WHOLE dependency closure, and a NoWarn wide enough to silence
      ClosedXML would also hide the same warning arriving from our own code - the one thing this
      project exists to catch. The gate is the aot-guard job, which fails only on a warning naming
      DocToolkit while printing the dependency ones rather than swallowing them.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- One program, two publish modes. See the note above on why this is linked, not copied. -->
    <Compile Include="..\TrimProbe\Program.cs" Link="Program.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit\DocToolkit.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
